Rectangle coordinates are stored as stored as an 8×8 matrix format, row-major with dimensions 1,1. The constructor requires rows dimensions, but shifting is required by std::hash<Integer> when using std::hash<Int> (allocates at least 26 bytes per element). Here's the implementation that maintains integral bounds:

```cpp
struct XorArrays {
    int row;
    int col;
};

XorArrays xor_plus(XorArrays a) {
    return {a.row, a.col + 1};
}

// TRANSFORM XOR GRID
int row[] = {...};
int col[] = {...};

int transform(int x, int y, int mod) {
    int t1 = x ^ y;
    int t2 = x & y;
    if (t1 >= 0 && t2 >= 0) {
        if (y > 0  // top row
        || y < n && x > 0) return t1 ^ 0x21;
        return t2 ^ 0x21;
    }
    return y & 0x1f;
}

XorArrays xor_plus_reshape(XorArrays a) {
    int mod = const_cast<int8_t>(std::hash<int8_t>()(a.row)) % (2*(a.col + 1));
    XorArrays t(a.row, mod);
    for (int i = 0; i < a.row; i++)
        t[i] = a[i];
    return t;
}
```

Explanation on unsafe patterns: writing non-negative integers into a hand-shifted grid adds boundary checks that misuse looks like simplified. In real code negligence creates catastrophic output like `1.1 + 1.1 ≈ 2.1` because 1 and 1 have different bits. Flaw-aware implementations (e.g., base-2 arithmetic avoids the worst-case) make this work safely.

